---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/overview_background.jpg";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A black background filled with cascading columns of green digital-rain characters — Latin letters, digits and Japanese kana and kanji, a few glowing bright white"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <PageLayout>
    <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
      {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2>What you will do</h2>
    <p>
      Each week engineers a piece of the malware production lifecycle:
      modelling a threat, designing an architecture, triggering execution,
      and analysing the artefact a defender is left to interpret. Lectures
      establish the concepts; the laboratory sessions and the two major
      assignments turn them into artefacts of their own, from a modular
      design blueprint through to a fully documented, fictional intrusion.
      Every exercise is confined to benign samples and simulated
      infrastructure, so that what a student produces is evidence of
      engineering judgement, not a functioning weapon.
    </p>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>
      The course assumes a working background in operating systems,
      networking and at least one general-purpose programming language, of
      the kind an undergraduate computing degree provides by its third
      year. No prior exposure to offensive security is assumed, although
      students who have read about malware informally will recognise the
      vocabulary from the first lecture onward. Anyone unsure whether their
      background is sufficient should treat Week 1's outline as the test:
      if most of it is unfamiliar, this course is likely a poor fit at this
      stage of a degree.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <CardGrid columns={2}>
      <Card title="Timeline" href={withBase("/timeline/")}>
        <p>Every graded deliverable, in semester order, on one spine.</p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="People" href={withBase("/people/")}>
        <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Policies" href={withBase("/policies/")}>
        <p>The rules that govern how the course is run and assessed.</p>
      </Card>
    </CardGrid>
  </PageLayout>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
